WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.230
TAD EINSTEIN: Welcome
to StratoZone,

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.850
the only way to start
your migration journey.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.090
My name is Tad Einstein,
and I'm a senior program

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.800
manager with Google Cloud.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.010
We also have Karl Koshy,
CEO of StratoZone here today

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.870
to talk about the
benefits of StratoZone

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.218
and how it will help you
accelerate your cloud journey.

00:00:22.218 --> 00:00:23.760
One of the first
pieces that you want

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.640
to focus on when looking
to migrate to the cloud

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.160
is the assessment
and discovery phase.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.430
Doing things proper early on
will yield amazing results

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.480
in the end.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.000
So one of the first
pieces is to make sure

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.100
that your team is properly
educated on the cloud

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.670
and that your engineers
are ready to actually move

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.160
to the cloud once you
start the process.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.280
So things like training
are critical in this space.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.160
Also, you'll want to
focus on experimentation.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.980
It's very important that they
get to break things in order

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.740
to understand how to get through
some very interesting cloud

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.320
paradigms during your migration.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.510
And, also during the
assessment phase,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.450
you need to catalog all
of your applications.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.210
So we typically create
three buckets, those which

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.250
are easy, hard, and can't move.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.310
But when doing this, how
do you go about sorting

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.690
your applications?

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.270
And also, how do you go
about picking a first mover?

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.360
In a typical enterprise,
there are many applications

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.530
which are interwoven.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.510
So doing this step
properly is paramount

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.050
to a successful migration.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.530
So as we discuss examples
of application sorting,

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:29.750
you can see that we have
those systems which might be

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.730
disconnected and non-critical.

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:34.110
This might be something
like a marketing website,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.485
back up an archive, or death
test environment where you're

00:01:37.485 --> 00:01:39.860
literally able to take them
from the current environment,

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.840
whether on premise
or another cloud

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.530
and lift and shift them into
Google Cloud very easily.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.720
You'll also then have your
set of applications that

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.730
fall under what we call hard.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.250
Those are things like
back office applications,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.022
data analytics.

00:01:54.022 --> 00:01:55.730
They're usually
non-critical, but they're

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.390
very interconnected.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.400
So those might not be
the best first mover,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.840
but they're perfect
for the cloud.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.310
And you might want to do those
in a second or third way.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.850
Then we also have those that
just cannot move to the cloud

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.060
that maybe running on legacy
hardware which just doesn't fit

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.303
in a cloud paradigm.

00:02:14.303 --> 00:02:15.970
So what are the
business characteristics

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.673
of a good first mover?

00:02:17.673 --> 00:02:19.090
Well, when you
think about it, you

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.160
want those applications
which are not

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.753
business critical and also
which are not edge cases.

00:02:24.753 --> 00:02:26.170
You want to find
applications that

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.210
are stateless that, as
we've said previously,

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.020
can literally be
lifted and shifted.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:31.660
And that makes a
great first mover

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.480
because it allows you to learn
the interworkings, not only

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.690
the cloud, but of the
migration process.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.080
You also focus on apps that do
not require much refactoring

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.640
for your first movers.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.400
It makes no sense spending a ton
of time having your developers

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.530
recode an app when there
are other applications

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.030
in your environment
that can quickly

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:50.950
be brought into the
cloud as you're working

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.110
through things like compliance.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.870
Also, be aware of those
applications which

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:59.200
do have compliance challenges
or third-party proprietary

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.850
licenses.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:02.980
When moving to the cloud
or any sort of migration,

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.290
you have to think
about cutover windows.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.240
So when choosing
that first mover,

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.800
make sure it's an
application where

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.750
you can accept that
downtime to your business

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.550
so that when you
lift and shift, it's

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.520
not of significant
impact to the business.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.530
And then most importantly,
throughout all

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.360
of these iterations,
you're going

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.280
to build a knowledge
base for your engineers

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.363
and for your team and for
your business as a whole

00:03:23.363 --> 00:03:25.390
as it relates to
the cloud migration.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.420
This is why it's critical to
find those right first movers

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.670
so that you can build a
knowledge base which you then

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.170
will build off of even further.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.190
So Google's created a cloud
migration methodology.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.390
And it consists of four stages.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.030
The first, which we
just referred to,

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.880
is the assessment phase,
which is where you understand

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.760
what's currently in your
environment, how it connects,

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.410
and what would be
needed when you start

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:49.510
looking to move to the cloud.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.958
Next, you have the
foundations phase.

00:03:51.958 --> 00:03:53.500
And this is where
you actually set up

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.640
the cloud for the first time.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.830
So what you do is you
create a landing zone.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:01.270
You set up networking,
identity and access management,

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.070
security rules that
are in compliance

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.590
with what your company desires.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.750
Once the foundation
is properly set up,

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:09.940
you can then go through
the migration phase, which

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.920
ensures that what
you're migrating

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.710
goes into an environment
that is properly sound based

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:17.630
on your company's objectives
and security model.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:20.200
Finally, the last
phase is optimizing.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.380
Once you've lifted and shifted
your applications to the cloud,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.350
now you can start optimizing
and leverage cloud native

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:29.950
technologies to really benefit
from what the cloud breaks.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.650
Now, I want to quickly touch
on the assessment phase

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.540
because this is often the
one where people get stuck.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.420
And the reason why is
actually understanding

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.790
what's in your environment
can be very difficult.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:41.830
Often, enterprises
have applications

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:44.080
that have spanned
upwards of 20 years.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.200
And often the information
is not readily available.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.270
By leveraging a
tool like StratoZone

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.300
you're able to easily deploy
their data collection utilities

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.030
within your environment,
collect the data of how

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.910
your applications interact,
review the analytics

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.450
and also optimize cloud strategy
of what those applications will

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:05.437
look like during the migration
and also create a business case

00:05:05.437 --> 00:05:06.895
and road map, which
you can present

00:05:06.895 --> 00:05:11.460
to your IT leadership
and corporate executives.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.700
Now, the five needs to
create a cloud strategy are

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.940
to have a scalable and
non-intrusive discovery process

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.090
or assessment phase, which is
what StratoZone brings to bear

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:24.990
as well as the ability to define
cloud fit and suitability.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.880
We discussed how not
every application

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.440
is a good fit for the cloud.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.940
And by leveraging
StratoZone, you're

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.390
able to quickly understand
which one of those applications

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:35.040
might need to be
retooled or might not

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.820
be a fit also defining the
shared consumption plan.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.700
So delivering a high savings,
a return on investment

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.090
within Google Cloud,
maybe understanding

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.590
that certain things that ran
in your on-premise environment,

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.020
you can adjust the
metrics of RAM CPU

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.050
to something a little less
and get significant savings

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.120
without a performance hit.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.480
Also, defensible and
competitive financials.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.400
So getting the information
that you're then

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.820
going to give to your CFO, to
your CEO that clearly defines

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.820
what your cloud strategy
is from a financial aspect

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.790
and what it looks like
over time and then also

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:10.435
a consumable roadmap.

00:06:10.435 --> 00:06:12.060
Now that you understand
the financials,

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.442
now that you understand
what's going to move,

00:06:14.442 --> 00:06:15.900
what does that look
like over time?

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.025
And how are we going to
get our entire organization

00:06:18.025 --> 00:06:19.055
into the cloud?

00:06:19.055 --> 00:06:20.430
So with that being
said, I'd like

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.710
to introduce Karl
Koshy, CEO of StratoZone

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.600
to talk more about
StratoZone and how

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:26.931
you can leverage it today.

00:06:26.931 --> 00:06:28.080
KARL KOSHY: Thanks, Tad.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.750
So we're going to spend a minute
talking about what StratoZone

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.980
delivers to the customer.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:35.730
There are two levels
of deliverables.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.530
First, we're talking
about the basics.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:41.250
The first set of deliverables
is around financials,

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.210
business case, and roadmap.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:44.810
So let's go through
some of those.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.940
Number one, StratoZone helps
you to very efficiently discover

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:48.720
your environment.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.760
This is an agentless
data collection process

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:52.350
that's very quick.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:55.020
After this is done,
StratoZone will then

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.030
capture the customer
requirements

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:00.360
which it overlays on the
underlying data, thereby

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.820
helping to pick the
best fit products

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.940
and ultimately arrive at
what the goes where and when.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.460
This is the crux of what
StratoZone delivers.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.560
That includes a
delivery model which

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.420
could determine how
you consume cloud

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:15.670
is this infrastructure as a
service, platform as a service

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:16.470
and so on.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.780
Or is something not meant to go
to cloud in your implementation

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:20.280
model?

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.580
So you decide in
your cloud model

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.790
such as your hybrid model, a
private, public, or otherwise

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.430
blended model that
you may want to use.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.910
After this, the platform helps
you develop your business case,

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.550
create your adoption
roadmap, and then

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:39.560
also create a migration plan.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.450
Some of the advanced
deliverables

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:47.120
include machine shaping,
optimization, roadmap modeling,

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:50.270
PaaS analysis, which is
platform as a service analysis,

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.190
and then most importantly,
dependency mapping.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:55.340
This is an important
component of what customers

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:58.520
look for in their environment
to see what talks to what,

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:01.530
and how all of your
applications are related.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.140
This is a very
critical component

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:06.440
needed when you decide this--
what of your environment

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.810
is going to move
first, second, last,

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:12.410
and also decide on
the timing for those.

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:15.500
With this, we'll go now into
how you can use StratoZone

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:20.070
and what the process looks
like to implement StratoZone.

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:23.660
So the first step in the
process is to register either

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:24.410
as a customer.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.450
Or a partner can
register their customer

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:28.325
and gogcp.stratozone.com.

00:08:28.325 --> 00:08:32.161
Once again, that's
gogcp.stratozone.com.

00:08:32.161 --> 00:08:35.640
The customer then receives
one or more data collectors,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.909
which are simple install
shield applications

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:40.220
that they will use to
install the application

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:41.659
in their environments.

00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:44.390
It could be one
collector per environment

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:46.640
or one collector across
multiple environments.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.415
This is the customer's choice.

00:08:48.415 --> 00:08:49.790
The second step
in the process is

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:51.980
where the customer's
technical administrators

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:54.950
deployed the data collectors
after customizing it

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:56.340
to their needs.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.120
This could include
deciding what IP ranges

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.280
they want to collect
upon or what environments

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:02.600
they want to collect upon.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:06.350
Once that collector is
deployed within 24 hours,

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:09.440
customers, partners, as
well as your cloud provider,

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.300
can come up there
and take a look

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.490
and see what those
results look like.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.210
This includes the analysis of
the environment, optimization

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:21.450
of assets, optimization
of your machine shapes

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:24.950
and how you would consume cloud,
as well as developing a roadmap

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:27.620
and moving on to the next
steps in your planning.

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:31.130
Now, the StratoZone data
collector is agentless.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:32.870
It is all encrypted.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:33.950
It's lightweight.

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:35.720
And most importantly,
customers can

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.640
decide what data
fields they want

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.940
to anonymous from within
their firewall that

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:43.070
reaches the StratoZone portal.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:45.050
So this is a custom
anonymization ability

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.570
given to customers.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.970
Let's take a second and
talk about data security

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:52.780
and the data that's collected.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:54.440
The data that's
collected primarily

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:56.030
focuses on machine data--

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:59.240
things like performance,
installed application names,

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.790
network traffic,
processes and services

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.730
that are running in
the utilization of all

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.470
of these components.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:09.800
What we do not collect is
user information or profiles,

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:13.460
any data that's underlying
the applications, anything

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.560
in your database
or file storage,

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.020
application configuration
files, for example,

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.600
or typically any
data that is subject

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:22.910
to any regulatory compliances.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:25.400
All the data is encrypted
in transit and at rest.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:27.850
And customers can
anonymize any data

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:30.560
they see fit from
leaving their firewall

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.952
and going to the
StratoZone portal.

