and finally, generating a
report showing your cloud fit

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.630
and business case.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.180
To get started, you will first
need to register your customer

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.845
at gogcp.stratozone.com.

00:11:38.845 --> 00:11:42.090
When you do so, you will also
receive a separate registration

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:43.860
link via email.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:45.970
Once you create your
username and password,

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.285
you'll be able to log in
at portal.stratozone.com

00:11:48.285 --> 00:11:52.210
and get to this dashboard
that I'm looking at now.

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:54.420
So once you're here,
how does StratoZone

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:58.030
help you and your customer on
the cloud migration journey?

00:11:58.030 --> 00:11:59.730
Well, we have two
different modules

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:01.560
associated with the assessment.

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.030
First is the Analyze module.

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.700
And this is where all of the
raw data from the environment

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:06.840
is collected.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.550
It's also where we
review that data

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.890
and validate that it's the
complete set of migration

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.410
candidates.

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:14.370
The Analyze module
will also allow

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:18.400
you to create an
initial business case.

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:21.300
The Plan module is here for
more advanced price modeling,

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:25.530
as this allows you to do custom
groupings, custom right sizing,

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:31.410
and also select particular
VM instances per server.

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:34.870
So let's start with
the Analyze module.

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:37.870
Let's start with
the Analyze module.

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:39.940
When you first click
on the Analyze module,

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:42.400
you'll be presented with
the Analyze Dashboard.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:45.370
In this dashboard, you can view
the total number of servers

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:47.063
collected into inventory.

00:12:47.063 --> 00:12:48.730
You can view the
number of those servers

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:50.860
that are Windows
and Linux and which

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:52.960
ones are marked out of scope.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:57.720
You can also view the total
virtual core count, the VRAM,

00:12:57.720 --> 00:12:59.790
and the allocated storage.

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:02.460
In the bottom right here,
you can view the server age

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:03.720
distribution.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:06.520
And in the top right, you
can view our StratoFit score,

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:11.030
which is a measure of
infrastructure cloud readiness.

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:15.070
Next, let's view
the Summary tab.

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:17.520
The Summary tab is still
taking an aggregate

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.520
of all customer servers.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:22.020
However, it does allow you to
view some metrics that are not

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.340
available in the Dashboard tab.

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:26.580
For instance, you can view
the exact operating system

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.220
distribution of the customer
environment, as well

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:32.220
as that environment's
memory distribution.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:35.460
In the bottom left, you can
view some basic asset statistics

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.620
about the environment.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:41.040
And in the bottom right, you
can view the asset utilization

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:45.860
as a percentage of the
total allocated capacity.

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:48.770
On the left side toolbar, you
can view additional details

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.960
about the operating systems, .

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:58.030
storage, memory, server age,
and the installed software

00:13:58.030 --> 00:13:59.320
within the environment.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:04.510
Of course, you won't be able
to view this information

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.820
until the scan is
actually running.

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:08.860
To view the progress
of the scan,

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:10.630
access the Assessments
tab at the top.

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:15.440
After you click on
Assessments, you'll

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.300
be presented with your
different assessment groups.

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:22.460
In this example, we have a group
for East coast, West coast,

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:24.980
primary data
center, and anything

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:26.870
that's not Windows
or Linux, which

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:30.370
must be a manual asset entry.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:33.490
As you can see, we have one
collector for each data center

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:34.960
location.

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:38.110
The general recommendation
is to have one per location.

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:39.580
But keep in mind
that the customer

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:43.670
can install more than one
per location if desired.

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:46.300
And any duplicate assets that
are scanned by both collectors

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:50.600
will be automatically
removed by the system.

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:52.510
Note that for each
collector, you

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:56.290
can check the status of the
scan as well as the server

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:59.560
count of the expected values.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:03.910
For the status, it will either
say complete or installed

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:05.800
if it's been started.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:08.230
Or it will say not
started if it has not

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:10.300
been installed and connected
to the web service.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.680
After the collectors are
installed and configured,

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:19.155
you'll be able to view the
inventory as it comes in.

00:15:19.155 --> 00:15:22.090
To do so, access the
Inventory tab at the top.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:29.150
Now, that we have access this
specific server, at the top,

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:32.030
you can see all of the
basic configuration info.

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:35.060
This will tell you not only
what is the server name and IP,

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:38.360
but also whether it's a virtual
machine or bare metal, how

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:42.410
many CPU cores are allocated,
how much RAM is allocated,

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:44.810
what operating system
it's running on,

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:48.920
as well as the processor
of the host machine.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:50.840
If you scroll down,
you can also view

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.570
graphs that outline
the performance data

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:56.320
or the utilization of each of
those components over time.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:02.260
Similar to the Summary
tab, more information

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:04.780
can be accessed on
the left hand toolbar.

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:07.390
For instance, clicking
on the Storage tab

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:09.640
will allow you to view details
about the exact storage

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:13.160
partitions, what's
free, what's not.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.670
If you click on Network, you
can view the exact network

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:17.860
configuration as
well as what subnet

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:20.800
that server is located in.

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:22.690
You can also view
running services

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:29.670
on that server, running
processes, installed software

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:34.980
if there are any, as well as
memory utilization metrics

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.470
and CPU utilization
metrics by process.

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:43.340
What I have just shown
you from the Inventory tab

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:46.100
represents all of the
configuration and performance

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:49.280
data that we have
collected from each server.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.740
The last piece of
information that we have

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:53.780
are the network dependencies.

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:55.220
To view the network
dependencies,

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:59.610
click on the Dependency
tab at the top.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:01.500
The Analyze
Dependency tab allows

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:04.260
you to view server-to-server
network dependencies

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:07.440
by selecting an
individual server.

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:09.420
To select that
server, you can do so

00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:11.790
by accessing your
collector groups

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:14.190
and a specific collector.

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:17.220
Or you can just click on
Details on the left-hand toolbar

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:20.430
to view all available servers.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:22.619
Once you've selected the
server that you want,

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:25.440
click on the eyeglasses
symbol to view it.

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:28.290
This will now provide you with
a graphical view of the network

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:32.710
dependencies, as well as a more
specific view in a table format

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:35.080
below.

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:38.700
As you can see, we're collecting
the protocol, source and target

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:42.150
IP, source and target port,
as well as the installed

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:44.610
app on the source machine.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:47.160
All of this information
comes from a netstat command

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:53.290
and is included as part of the
agentless scan via StratoProbe.

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:56.200
One critical feature of
this tab is its ability

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:59.220
to detect on scan devices.

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:01.140
You can see an example
of this down here

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:03.280
where it says Unscanned Device.

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:05.970
If I expand that, you can
see the same netstat details

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:09.010
about that unscanned device.

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:12.402
You can also view that device
in a graphical format up here.

00:18:12.402 --> 00:18:13.860
There is an Unscanned
Device button

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.560
that will bundle any
unscanned devices.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:19.650
If you double-click that and
click this in the top right

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:22.620
and in the view, you can
see that there are actually

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:25.300
two distinct IP addresses.

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:27.580
Now, we have not
scanned these devices.

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:31.110
And so the only thing that we
know about them are their IPs.

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:34.500
But this is still, nonetheless,
useful for a scoping exercise

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:36.930
to ensure that all
target servers are

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:40.100
being included in the analysis.

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:42.540
The next tab we will look
at is the StratoFit tab.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:47.820
The StratoFit tab is a
machine-level indicator

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:49.500
of cloud readiness.

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:52.110
It does not take into
account broader server side

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.130
application structure.

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:56.640
However, it does look at
each individual server

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:00.630
and determine based on that
service capacity and operating

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:04.720
system, whether it is ready for
a lift and shift to the cloud.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:07.540
As you can see, the
StratoFit is represented

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.280
by three different scores--

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:11.776
low, medium, and high.

00:19:11.776 --> 00:19:13.600
High are the easy movers.

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:16.720
These are the servers that are
already modernized and ready

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:18.940
for lift and shift.

00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:21.730
Lows are anything that is
incompatible for the cloud

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:22.750
entirely.

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:26.380
Usually this is due to an
older operating system, say,

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:29.500
an old CentOS version,
like CentOS 4 or 5,

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:33.850
or Windows 2000/2003 that
are deemed incompatible

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:36.440
for the cloud.

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:38.120
The servers that
are flagged medium

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:40.490
are somewhere in
between where they

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:43.070
are technically modernized
enough for the cloud.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:44.540
However, they tend
to have a higher

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:47.360
number of network dependencies
with other servers.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.120
This means that they
are most likely involved

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:53.900
in some type of broader, more
complicated app structure that

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:57.220
may make it more
difficult to move.

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:58.930
If you would like
to investigate why

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.900
a particular server received
the score that it did,

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:05.970
you can do so on the left-hand
toolbar under details.

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:08.190
If you click on Details,
it will list out

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:12.450
all of the scored servers
from low to medium to high.

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:14.460
If you want to know
why a server received

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:16.770
a score other than
high, simply click

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:19.770
on the dropdown for that
server and view those reasons

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:20.700
in sentence format.

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:27.860
Note that the StratoFit score
has six different parameters.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:30.410
You can customize how sensitive
each of these parameters

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:33.460
are on the left-hand
toolbar under Actions,

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:36.070
Customize Parameters.

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:38.000
If you click on
Customize Parameters,

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.240
you will see all six
of them listed out.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:42.100
And you can adjust
the slider bar

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:46.100
to change the
sensitivity of each.

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:48.530
To finish up the Analyze
module, let's take

00:20:48.530 --> 00:20:51.320
a look at the Reports tab.

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:53.510
Within the Reports tab,
it's important to note

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:56.510
that any of the information
contained in the Analyze module

00:20:56.510 --> 00:20:59.260
can be exported as a report.

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:02.440
At the top are summary reports
which tend to be either slide

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:04.780
shows or PDFs.

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:06.970
At the bottom are
detailed reports,

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:12.030
which are essentially CSV
dumps of the raw information.

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:15.480
To generate an Executive
Summary Report slideshow,

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:18.050
create a summary report.

00:21:18.050 --> 00:21:20.510
First, click on the
button for that,

00:21:20.510 --> 00:21:23.960
then select the groups, whether
collectors or bill groups

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:26.429
that you would like to
include in the slideshow.

00:21:31.273 --> 00:21:32.940
You can then select
your Baseline Vendor

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:39.040
Catalog, which we will usually
use the PRIVATE DATA CENTER.

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:45.060
Compare that to the
various Google catalogs,

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:50.900
name it, and click
Create Report.

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:56.790
After your report is
finished building,

00:21:56.790 --> 00:22:00.190
you can download it
by clicking this icon.

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:02.260
Note that you can download
it as a Google slides

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:03.610
as well if you prefer.

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:08.180
Now, let's open this
up so we can take

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:10.773
a look at what is produced.

00:22:10.773 --> 00:22:13.190
So for starters, it will produce
some introductory slides,

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:15.740
like a table of
contents, as well as

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:18.980
a background on the
overall assessment process.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:20.750
It will also produce
a slide outlining

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:24.070
the different collectors
that were set up.

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:26.800
Then it's going to jump right
into the high-level findings.

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:28.500
So this is the
high-level configuration

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:32.370
here followed by some
more advanced metrics,

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:37.830
like the utilization
percentage in the bottom right

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:40.260
and then jumping into
the cloud readiness.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:43.380
So this is the StratoFit score,
which is an infrastructure

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:46.530
cloud readiness score.

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:48.560
And we also have
a PaaS fit score.

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:54.887
So this is going to identify
any potential PaaS candidates.

00:22:54.887 --> 00:22:56.470
After those high-level
findings, we'll

00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:58.488
show the financial comparison.

00:22:58.488 --> 00:23:00.280
And this is what that's
going to look like.

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:05.263
After covering the
financial comparison,

00:23:05.263 --> 00:23:06.930
there will be a couple
of slides talking

00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:09.040
about the high-level next steps.

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:11.692
And this is intended to lead
into the next conversation

00:23:11.692 --> 00:23:12.650
for an advance service.

00:23:15.856 --> 00:23:18.330
TAD EINSTEIN: Thanks so
much for that demo, Karl--

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:19.675
really impressive.

00:23:19.675 --> 00:23:21.300
And with that being
said, I just wanted

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:24.420
to recall for everyone who's
watching that you can also

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.720
do exactly what Karl just did in
a full hands-on lab environment

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:31.140
prior to deploying StratoZone
within your environment.

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:33.630
To do so and take the
Introduction to StratoZone

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.960
Assessments hands-on lab,
follow the first link

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:40.680
to register for access to Google
Cloud Learning's platforms

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:43.110
and then click on
the following link

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:45.480
so that you can start the
lab and actually leverage

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:48.840
StratoZone within a real
world hands-on lab environment

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:52.950
prior to actually deploying it
in your enterprise environment.

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.380
With that being said, Karl
and I would both to thank you

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:56.500
for your time.

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:58.615
We look forward to seeing
you on Google Cloud.

00:23:58.615 --> 00:24:00.990
And we hope you really enjoy
the benefits that StratoZone

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:02.470
will bring to your business.

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:03.622
Thank you.

00:24:03.622 --> 00:24:05.720
KARL KOSHY: Thank you.

